//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
// An `Option<T>` holds its `T` by value. So a struct that holds an `Option` of itself has an
// infinite size. This holds directly, through a second struct, and for a generic struct. The
// compiler crashed in the drop checker on each. Each now gets an error at its struct, in a
// normal compile and in a parallel compile (`-j`).
//
// RUN: not vxc %s 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --implicit-check-not=Error
// RUN: not vxc -j 2 %s 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --implicit-check-not=Error
// CHECK: Error[E3070] at {{.*}}structs_that_hold_themselves_through_option.vx:22:8: `Node` holds itself by value, so its size is infinite: field `next` of `Node` holds `Option<Node>`, variant `Some` of `Option<Node>` holds `Node`. Hold the value through a pointer, for example `Box<Node>`
// CHECK: Error[E3070] at {{.*}}structs_that_hold_themselves_through_option.vx:26:8: `A` holds itself by value, so its size is infinite: field `b` of `A` holds `Option<B>`, variant `Some` of `Option<B>` holds `B`, field `a` of `B` holds `A`. Hold the value through a pointer, for example `Box<A>`
// CHECK: Error[E3070] at {{.*}}structs_that_hold_themselves_through_option.vx:33:8: `List<T>` holds itself by value, so its size is infinite: field `tail` of `List<T>` holds `Option<List<T>>`, variant `Some` of `Option<List<T>>` holds `List<T>`. Hold the value through a pointer, for example `Box<List<T>>`

import core::option;
import core::result;

struct Node {
  next: Option<Node>
}

struct A {
  b: Option<B>
}
struct B {
  a: A
}

struct List<T> {
  head: T,
  tail: Option<List<T>>
}

fn main() -> i32 {
  let r = Result<u64, Node>::Ok(5u64);
  let l = Result<u64, List<i32>>::Ok(5u64);
  print(1);
  return 0;
}
